    <figcaption>The cylinder lab — alternating vortices shedding into the wake, marked laminar and validated.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>The airfoil</h3>

<p>Tilt an airfoil into the stream and the air does the work of flight: faster over the top, slower beneath, turned downward as it leaves the trailing edge. That turning is lift. Raise the angle of attack and the field answers, with the lift and drag coefficients tracking live alongside — the link between what you see and what the wing feels, never abstract.</p>

<figure class="shot screen">
    <img src="/assets/img/lab-airfoil.png" width="1398" height="1048"
         alt="The airfoil lab: flow turning over an airfoil set at a positive angle of attack, with live lift and drag coefficients in the readout" loading="lazy">
    <figcaption>The airfoil lab — the flow bending over a section at angle of attack, lift and drag read out live.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>The nozzle</h3>

<p>A converging–diverging nozzle — the de Laval shape behind every rocket and wind tunnel — trades pressure for speed. The flow squeezes through the throat, accelerates, and the color map lifts from cool to bright as it goes: where the passage narrows, the flow must hurry through.</p>

<figure class="shot screen">
    <img src="/assets/img/lab-nozzle.png" width="1398" height="1048"
         alt="The nozzle lab: flow accelerating through the throat of a converging–diverging de Laval nozzle, shown as a velocity heat map brightening toward the centre" loading="lazy">
    <figcaption>The nozzle lab — flow accelerating through the throat of a de Laval nozzle, brightening as it speeds up.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>How the picture forms</h2>

<p>The flow you see is being computed, not played back. The app solves the equations of moving air thousands of times a second, and each pass refines the one before it. When you first change the speed, the opening moments are a rough first approximation; with every further pass the solution converges &mdash; it reproduces the real physics more and more closely, until the picture settles into what the air actually does. It is the same idea as a photograph developing: indistinct at first, then sharp and true. Nothing here is pre-rendered.</p>

<p class="meta">A beautiful, credible visualization of airflow &mdash; not a certified engineering tool, and not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored by any vehicle manufacturer.</p>

